This code checks the user agent string sent by the browser and executes browser-specific code accordingly. However, it's important to note that browser detection is not a reliable way to handle browser compatibility issues, as it can be inaccurate and may not work for future browser versions. Instead, it's recommended to use feature detection and progressive enhancement to ensure compatibility across all browsers.

To arrange the pieces on a chessboard with more than two queens, ensuring that no queen can capture another queen and at least one pawn of each color is present on the board, you can follow these steps:
1. Place the first queen on the board, anywhere you like.
2. Place the second queen on the board, making sure it is not in the same row, column, or diagonal as the first queen.
3. Place the first pawn on the board, in a row and column that is not occupied by a queen.
4. Place the third queen on the board, making sure it is not in the same row, column, or diagonal as the first two queens.
5. Place the second pawn on the board, in a row and column that is not occupied by a queen or the first pawn.
6. Place the fourth queen on the board, making sure it is not in the same row, column, or diagonal as the first three queens.
7. Place the third pawn on the board, in a row and column that is not occupied by a queen or either of the first two pawns.
8. Continue placing queens and pawns in this manner until the board is filled.
Here is an example SQL database code to automate this process for a chessboard of size 8x8 and 8 queens:
```
CREATE TABLE chessboard (
    row INT,
    column INT,
    piece VARCHAR(10)
);
INSERT INTO chessboard (row, column, piece)
VALUES (1, 1, 'queen');
DECLARE @i INT;
SET @i = 2;
WHILE @i <= 8
BEGIN
    DECLARE @j INT;
    SET @j = 1;
    WHILE @j <= 8
    BEGIN
        DECLARE @is_valid BIT;
        SET @is_valid = 1;
        DECLARE @k INT;
        SET @k = 1;
        WHILE @k < @i AND @is_valid = 1
        BEGIN
            IF EXISTS (
                SELECT *
                FROM chessboard
                WHERE row = @k
                    AND (column = @j OR ABS(column - @j) = @i - @k)
            )
            BEGIN
                SET @is_valid = 0;
            END;
            SET @k = @k + 1;
        END;
        IF @is_valid = 1
        BEGIN
            INSERT INTO chessboard (row, column, piece)
            VALUES (@i, @j, 'queen');
            BREAK;
        END;
        SET @j = @j + 1;
    END;
    SET @i = @i + 1;
END;
INSERT INTO chessboard (row, column, piece)
VALUES (1, 2, 'pawn'), (2, 1, 'pawn');
DECLARE @l INT;
SET @l = 3;
WHILE @l <= 8
BEGIN
    DECLARE @m INT;
    SET @m = 1;
    WHILE @m <= 8
    BEGIN
        IF NOT EXISTS (
            SELECT *
            FROM chessboard
            WHERE row = @l
                AND column = @m
        )
        BEGIN
            INSERT INTO chessboard (row, column, piece)
            VALUES (@l, @m, 'pawn');
            BREAK;
        END;
        SET @m = @m + 1;
    END;
    SET @l = @l + 2;
END;
SELECT *
FROM chessboard;
```
This code uses a while loop to place the queens and pawns on the board in a symmetrical pattern, while checking that no queen can capture another queen. The algorithm can be easily modified to work with different board sizes and numbers of queens.

